.TH "MAW-SECRETS" "7" "" "maw 0.2.4" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-secrets \- encrypted files, so the repo can be public
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Files with passwords or tokens, like \fBrclone.conf\fR, can live in the repo encrypted, so it can be public:
.PP
.EX
maw add --secret ~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf   # encrypted into static/rclone/rclone.conf.age
maw secret edit ~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf    # decrypted into your editor, encrypted back
maw secret rekey                                # re-encrypted for every machine, after adding one
.EE
.PP
.EX
record hosts/laptop.pub
encrypt ~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf -> static/rclone/rclone.conf.age
.EE
.PP
Files are encrypted with age (https://age-encryption.org) to your SSH key: \fB~/.ssh/id_ed25519\fR, else \fB~/.ssh/id_rsa\fR, or \fBmaw.secretKey = "~/.ssh/other";\fR in \fBconfig.nix\fR. Each machine's public key is in the repo as \fBhosts/<name>.pub\fR (written the first time a machine encrypts), and every file is encrypted to all of them, so each machine decrypts with its own key and no private key leaves its machine. It needs \fBage\fR (\fBmaw install age\fR); maw itself doesn't depend on it.
.PP
On activation, an encrypted file is decrypted into \fB~/.local/state/maw/secrets/\fR (readable only by you) and the live file links to that copy; the plaintext never enters the repo. It's decrypted again only when the encrypted file changes, so a key with a passphrase is asked for rarely; \fBstatus\fR, \fBdiff\fR, and dry runs never decrypt. If decrypting fails (a mistyped passphrase, age missing), the last decrypted copy stays linked and activation notes why; it's tried again next time. Edits made through the live file stay until the encrypted file next changes; then the edited copy is moved to the backups dir, and activation says where. \fBmaw secret edit\fR is how an edit reaches the repo.
.PP
A new machine can't decrypt anything until it's a recipient. Its first activation skips encrypted files with a note (\fBskip static/rclone/rclone.conf.age: ... isn't encrypted for this machine\fR). To add it: run \fBmaw secret rekey\fR there, which records its key as \fBhosts/<name>.pub\fR (and warns about each file it can't open yet), push; then \fBmaw secret rekey\fR and push on a machine that can decrypt, and pull on the new one.
.PP
